WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.380
Democrats are making some major mistakes right now,

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:05.720
really just completely unforced errors.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.700
And I want to dig into this a little bit.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:14.100
As I mentioned earlier this week, we should really be in the golden era of resurgence for Democrats.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.800
And it is instead just bumbling foolishness

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:24.900
and already telegraphing the same mistakes for 2020 that we saw Democrats make in 2018.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:30.260
So, to set the stage for you, we had the DNC disaster of 2016, right?

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:37.960
Absolute and total train wreck, interfering in the primary, resignations, really bad for Democrats, Inc.,

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.860
or what I like to call corporate democrats.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:46.120
And then I told you last week that there are Democratic donors that are reportedly excited

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:49.180
about California senator, Kamala Harris.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:54.780
And they're starting to give her money for the 2020 presidential race.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:01.260
And I said, "you know, no offense, she seems smart, seems like a possibly interesting candidate.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.500
But we've got the midterms coming up here, right?"

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:09.820
And anytime I hear that Democratic donors are excited about someone three years out,

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:14.420
I start to get worried since that's exactly what got us into this mess in the first place in 2016.

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:19.480
Cut to Monday, Democrats announced a new slogan, called, "a better deal."

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.600
I thought it was a disastrous slogan.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:27.760
A better deal could be sort of pushing back against Trump, who talks about how he will make the best deals.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:33.500
But it felt vacuous, and it already sort of conceded the concept of deals to Donald Trump.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:38.420
Others said, "You know, David, it might be a reference to FDR's New Deal."

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:42.900
But yet again, a better deal?  Better isn't even necessarily good.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:47.060
It could be better, but still bad, right? 
 So it's a totally weak slogan.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:52.280
And the substance is also really weak. And again, just to catch you up on where we were on Monday,

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:59.100
this new, "better deal" from Democrats is going to target job training programs, which Donald Trump has talked about.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:03.140
And it's not exactly what's going to get people super excited about voting for you.

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:08.920
Democrats want to renegotiate trade deals, which plays right into what Donald Trump has been talking about.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.360
And it gets you into the weeds of,

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:15.720
"How will we renegotiate trade deals versus how will they renegotiate trade deals?"

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:22.960
Democrats putting out, as part of this better deal, more specific spending plans for infrastructure,

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.300
which also, infrastructure is all well and good and with peace and love.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:32.900
But Trump has been talking about infrastructure deals, of course in a totally cronyistic vulture capitalist way.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:35.840
But it again gets Dems into the weeds, having to be like,

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:41.200
"Well, our infrastructure spending plan is better than Trump's infrastructure spending plan."

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.020
So big win really for anybody who believes in Trump's worldview.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:49.420
Because Democrats, with this better deal, essentially said,

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:53.680
"Hey, we're basically conceding that Trump is talking about all the right issues.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:03:00.040
But we're going to explain to you how our plans will actually be better than Donald Trump's plans."

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.200
So, this is where we were up until Monday.

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:13.740
Now, it's been reported that Senator Harris, Kamala Harris,  just did a second tour through the Hamptons of New York

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:17.760
to meet with so-called influential Democratic donors.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.240
I'll remind you that, earlier this month,

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:25.140
she met with Liz Robbins, who's a top Hillary Clinton bundler of donations.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:30.620
She was back in the Hamptons this weekend to meet more top Clinton donors.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:38.460
One anonymous Democratic donor said to The Hill that Harris is, quote, "absolutely going to run for President in 2020."

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:42.880
Another source said to page six that, quote, "she's on overdrive.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:48.040
It's not just about the money.  That's the easy part for her.  She wants to meet the influential."

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:54.340
She's reportedly already hired multiple former Clinton staffers.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:04:02.860
And she's also seemingly trying to circumvent what could be a Bernie Sanders-style challenger popping up in 2020.

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:08.300
She reportedly paid $400,000 to a company called Revolution Messaging,

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:13.320
which is the same firm that built Bernie Sanders's digital and branding strategy

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:18.260
while he was basically experiencing a media blackout back in 2015.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:24.540
And once again, it's this centrist, moderate, non-starter ideology.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:32.980
The Guardian, Pat, put out an op-ed a couple days ago saying that, Harris has a great centrist platform.

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:36.660
No, no, no!  Didn't we learn from 2016?

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:41.120
And then The Hill published an op-ed by a Democratic consultant and lobbyist, saying,

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:48.960
Clinton lost in 2012 because she was moderate, and conservative voters supported Barack Obama more.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:58.620
Okay, the problem in 2016 was partially that Hillary Clinton was to the right of Barack Obama, more centrist;

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:00.920
and, in part, lots of other problems as well.

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:02.720
They're going in the wrong direction here.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:08.740
I mean, it's 2017, and the Democratic Party is already setting themselves up to lose the next presidential election.

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:11.060
You know, I think because Trump is so scandal-ridden,

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:14.580
and because the Republicans in Congress are so ineffectual,

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.140
It gives this idea that maybe you can just stay the course,

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:19.100
that you don't have to go on the offense.

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:21.680
But we've learned from 2016 that that's not the case,

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:24.860
because Hillary Clinton primarily ran an anti-Trump campaign.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:25.580
DAVID:  Correct.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:30.960
She didn't promote any ideas of economic populism that poll well for broader Americans

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:35.000
that could really get you to the White House, or just to win majorities in Congress.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:36.680
That's the path that we have to take.

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:41.900
The center approach basically means compromise right now, right?

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:50.080
Center is, for example, when Barack Obama starts negotiating Obamacare with maybe a public option might be nice.

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:56.240
And we get nothing except tens of millions of new customers for private, for-profit insurance companies.

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:01.960
And, I always like to say this, Obamacare did some good things.  I've praised it for the good things it did.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:04.040
And I've criticized it for the bad things that it did.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:08.819
This will get us, I mean, maybe I'm joking here, President Ted Nugent or something equivalent,

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:13.460
maybe President Kid Rock, who's running for something up in Michigan I believe right now.

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:17.700
This is the type of thing that will get Trump re-elected or even worse.

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:20.960
And I'm not saying I'm against Kamala Harris, right?

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:25.560
I mean, I'm not necessarily against her.  But to anoint her now,

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:33.500
with this idea of Democratic establishment donors, and the DNC, and Democrats, Inc., saying,

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:37.860
"Hey, here's who we're going to go with around this centrist ideology."

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:42.880
We're starting to go about 2020 the exact same way that we went about 2016.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:45.760
We're seeing the same mistakes that we saw in 2016.

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:51.960
And, by the way, midterms 2018.  Why are we talking about 2020 right now?

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:56.280
Right.  It could be hard to win a battle of personalities against Donald Trump especially.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:00.600
And I think that's why, for the meantime, we should just focus on specific policy proposals.

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:05.100
Make a Venn diagram of the issues that are most important to the American people

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:07.100
and those that people will vote on.

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:11.860
So, for example, a broader economic populist message of things like raising the minimum wage, for example;

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:15.300
maybe reforming trade deals.  All of these things will get people to the polls.

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:19.220
And you really have to think about what issues are non-starters.

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:23.640
And this is difficult, right?  I mean we looked at that Gallup most important problem poll,

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:27.180
where they asked Americans, "What's the most serious or pressing issue?"

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:32.260
And we saw generally the biggest issue is distrust and disapproval of our elected officials:

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:34.300
just people don't really trust our elected officials.

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:38.260
That's a big Matzo ball hanging out there that you've got to deal with in some way.

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:42.400
Healthcare was at the absolute top of that list.  And I've been saying it for months now,

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:48.120
Democrats need to just say, "We're for Medicare-for-all.  We've got a bill.  It's HR 676."

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:51.900
It doesn't matter that it won't pass with a Republican House, Senate, and White House.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:54.080
But say that it's what you're for.

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:58.980
This whole thing of, "Well, Obamacare has some issues, which we should fix.

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:03.940
But we never actually will.  But we don't want to repeal it.  And the Republican replacement, ba ba ba ba ba."

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:11.380
"We're for health care for everybody. 
 Here's how we pay for it.  It is our moral and patriotic duty.

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:18.020
We will be the best country when it comes to tech and engineering."  And the environment and whatever.

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:23.000
If people don't have to worry about, "Am I gonna lose my health care if I change jobs?"

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:25.499
You've got to do that.  And then you have to consider

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:31.460
what issues sort of show up as important but may not be as important of a voting issue.

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:37.900
So immigration is one that is often debated.  You could say, "Well, no.  People do vote on immigration policy,

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:41.780
because Donald Trump talked about banning Muslims, and people loved it.  And he won."

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:44.740
At the same time, though, you have to think about,

00:08:44.780 --> 00:08:51.040
will a more reasonable immigration position from Democrats actually get people out and voting?

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:54.600
If not, maybe you don't make it a huge part of your platform.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:58.100
When you talk about what issues are most important to Americans right now,

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:01.580
you may be surprised to learn that Russia doesn't rank very high on that list.

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:06.720
And while that is important - I think because it goes to, you know, the integrity of our electoral system,

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:09.820
whether or not we have an illegitimate president as people often say -

00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:10.380
DAVID: Of course.

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:12.500
maybe not mention it on the campaign trail as much?

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:17.459
Yeah.  And, as I've said before, if Russia is something big and it pulls Trump down, Trump will be gone.

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:20.880
You can't run against Trump.  If Russia evaporates and is nothing,

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:25.500
you still need something else to run against Trump or Republicans in 2018 and in 2020.

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:30.740
And what I can assure you is this, again, idea of, "Let's run in the middle!"

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:36.660
This is a recipe for disaster.  And Democrats are already failing on this.

